{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And Jacob went out.</b> Because on account of the daughters of Canaan being evil in the eyes of Isaac his father, Esau went to Ishmael, Scripture interrupted the matter of Jacob's pericope and wrote ״וַיַּרְא עֵשָׂו כִּי בֵּרַךְ״ and so on — and once it finished, it returned to the first matter." "<b>And he went out.</b> There was no need to write anything but ״וַיֵּלֶךְ יַעֲקֹב חָרָנָה״ — so why mention his departure? Rather, it teaches that the departure of a righteous person from a place makes an impression: while the righteous person is in the city, he is its splendor, he is its radiance, he is its majesty; he departed from there — its splendor departed, its radiance departed, its majesty departed. And likewise ״וַתֵּצֵא מִן הַמָּקוֹם״, stated regarding Naomi and Ruth (Ruth 1)." "<b>And he went to Haran.</b> He set out to go to Haran."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:00:56.516037036Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}